<meta type=article title="Code indentation — authored code renders verbatim through a code block (Option A)">
</meta>

<section | Authored code renders verbatim>

An author's indentation in code is never disturbed. enscribe guarantees this
through the **code block** — the fenced sigil `<``` … ```>` or `<code-block>` —
which renders its content as `<pre><code>`, byte-for-byte, indentation intact. A
bare `<code>` is for *inline* code, like the name <code | factorial> in this
sentence, and by nature carries no multi-line indentation.

<sub-section | A code block from a stored dataset>

`<code-block src="@factorial">` pulls a stored `<dataset>` and renders it through
the whitespace-safe `<pre><code>` path. The nested indentation of the function
below survives exactly as written — the 4-space body and the 8-space loop line:

<code-block src="@factorial" />

<data>
<dataset #factorial python>
def factorial(n):
    result = 1
    for i in range(1, n + 1):
        result = result * i
    return result
</dataset>
</data>
